  "text": "To find the value of \\( b \\) such that the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\) has at least two distinct integer roots, we start by considering the roots of the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\). Let's denote the roots of this polynomial by \\( r_1, r_2, r_3 \\). According to Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots taken one at a time is equal to the negation of the coefficient of \\( x^2 \\) divided by the coefficient of \\( x^3 \\), and so on. For the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -3. \\]\n\nNext, we need to consider the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\). Since it has the same constant term (7) as the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\), we need to investigate how the roots of the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\) factor into the roots of the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + 7 = 0 \\). \n\nSuppose the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\) has two distinct integer roots, say \\( r_1 \\) and \\( r_2 \\). Then the polynomial must also be expressible in a form that allows for integer roots. Given that the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\) has integer roots, let's assume the roots are \\( p, q, r \\) such that \\( pqr = 7 \\). The simplest assumption for the integer roots is that one of them is 7 (because 7 is a prime), and the other two are 1 and 1. So, we can set \\( a = p + q + r - 3 = 7 + 1 + 1 - 3 = 6 \\). To maintain the constant term as 7, we need to check if the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + 6y + 7 = 0 \\) can have two distinct roots of the form \\( x = 7 - y \\).\n\nLet's substitute \\( y = 7 - x \\) into the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + 6x + 7 = 0 \\) and see if we can find integer roots. We know that one root must be 7 (since 7 is a factor of the constant term 7), so let's try substituting \\( y = 7 \\) and \\( y = -1 \\):\n\nFor \\( y = 7 \\):\n\\[ 343 + 3 \\cdot 49 + 6 \\cdot 7 + 7 = 7 + 49 + 42 + 7 = 105 \\neq 0. \\]\n\nFor \\( y = -1 \\):\n\\[ 1 - 3 + 6 - 7 = 1 - 9 = -8 \\neq 0. \\]\n\nSince neither substitution works, we need to explore another approach. The simplest assumption is that the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + 7 = 0 \\) has roots 7 and 1. Let's assume \\( r_1 = 7 \\) and \\( r_2 = 1 \\), then \\( r_3 = -1 \\). This gives us the coefficient \\( a = 6 \\) and we need to find \\( b \\). The polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + 6y + b = 0 \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ b = - (7 + 1 - 1) = -6 + 3 = 12. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( b \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{49}\n\\]",
